unit Kafka.ZstdTests;

{ Tests for the zstd decoder (Kafka.Compression.Zstd) and XXH64; the batch
  the Java producer compressed with zstd is in Kafka.ProduceFetchTests.

  - XXH64: the values of the xxhash package for Python, with seeds, and
    lengths across its 32-, 8-, 4- and 1-byte steps.
  - Frames written by the reference library (python zstandard over libzstd
    1.5.7, the libzstd of Java's zstd-jni 1.5.7-17), chosen so that between
    them they use every part of the format a compressor writes: raw, RLE and
    compressed blocks; raw and Huffman literals, in 1 and 4 streams, their
    weights written directly and with FSE, the previous block's tree reused;
    predefined, RLE, FSE and repeated tables for literal lengths, offsets
    and match lengths, and a sequence whose literal and match lengths both
    take extra bits (the order they are read in); 1- and 2-byte sequence
    counts; a window descriptor
    or a single segment, content sizes of 1, 2 and 4 bytes, a checksum; and
    a frame written as Java's producer writes it (a stream of unknown size,
    level 3). Each comment says which.
  - Frames built by hand for what no compressor wrote here: RLE literals, a
    3-byte sequence count (33 000 sequences), an 8-byte content size, a
    dictionary id of 0, single sequences with RLE tables. Every one was
    decoded by libzstd to the same bytes while this was written.
  - Several frames and skippable frames in one payload, as Java reads them.
  - What Java's decoder refuses: a dictionary, a window above 128 MiB (128
    MiB itself read), zstd 0.x's legacy frames, a reserved bit. And corrupt
    frames and blocks, each refused with the reason in the message, every
    one refused by libzstd too (checked while this was written): cut short
    anywhere, bytes after the last frame, the reserved block type, a block
    above its frame's maximum, a content size or checksum that doesn't
    match, a match into the frame before, too few literals for 4 Huffman
    streams, Huffman weights that make no code, an FSE table above its
    accuracy log, an RLE symbol out of range, reserved bits, a bitstream
    with bits left over or too few, literals or tables missing.
  - The limit: refused from the frame header when the frame says its size,
    while decoding when it doesn't.
  - Corrupt input (bytes changed, cut, inserted) either decodes or raises
    EKafkaCompression, never anything else. The same, many times over and
    with the unit built with range checks on, ran while this was written.
  - zstd through Kafka.Compression: read, not written; a record batch whose
    records are a zstd frame decodes to its records.

  The vectors are hex constants made with tools/codec_vectors.py zstd;
  Text, Pseudorandom, Letters and LowBytes make the same inputs here as that
  script.

  DUnitX master: the FPCUnit mirror in tests/Unit/fpc is generated by
  tools/gen_fpc_mirror.py. }

interface

uses
  DUnitX.TestFramework,
  Kafka.DUnitXCompat,
  SysUtils,
  Kafka.Types,
  Kafka.Compression,
  Kafka.Compression.Zstd,
  Kafka.Protocol.Crc32c,
  Kafka.Protocol.RecordBatch,
  Kafka.TestMessages,
  Kafka.TestHelpers;

type
  [TestFixture]
  TKafkaZstdTests = class
  public
    [Test] procedure XxHash64_KnownVectors;
    [Test] procedure ReadsTheReferenceFrames;
    [Test] procedure ReadsHandBuiltFrames;
    [Test] procedure SeveralFrames_AndSkippableFrames;
    [Test] procedure WhatJavaRefuses_IsRefused;
    [Test] procedure CorruptFrames_AreRefused;
    [Test] procedure CorruptBlocks_AreRefused;
    [Test] procedure LargerThanTheLimit_IsRefused;
    [Test] procedure CorruptInput_IsDecodedOrRefused_NothingElse;
    [Test] procedure ThroughKafkaCompression_ReadNotWritten;
  end;

implementation

